            <ArticleTitle>Supportive care in lung cancer: clinical update.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e1-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.soncn.2012.03.009</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present a clinical update regarding common distressing lung cancer symptoms and provide an update on management interventions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Journal articles, systematic reviews.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Goals of treatment of the patient with lung cancer must include management of the high symptom burden that often accompanies the disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPLICATIONS FOR NURSING PRACTICE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Early assessment and management of symptoms improves quality of life. Nurses play a key role in implementing and monitoring these interventions.</AbstractText>
